# ACloudViewer Plugin Development Rules

## Plugin Architecture

- Plugins live under `plugins/core/Standard/` (standard) or `plugins/core/IO/` (I/O filters)
- Each plugin needs: `CMakeLists.txt`, `info.json`, a `.qrc` resource file
- Use `AddPlugin(NAME ...)` from `plugins/cmake/Plugins.cmake`
- Plugins link: `CVCoreLib`, `CVPluginAPI`, `CVPluginStub`

## AICore-Based Plugins (mandatory)

Read `.agents/skills/acloudviewer-aicore-plugin/SKILL.md` for the complete
integration workflow and acceptance gates, especially its `references/`
documents for reuse ownership and executable accuracy/performance profiles.

- First decide whether the plugin consumes an existing task, extends a model
  family, or requires a genuinely new input/output/lifecycle contract. A new UI
  or product name alone does not justify a new AICore task.
- The plugin is a thin Qt/application adapter: input/source selection, worker
  invocation, annotations/DB entities, rendering, and export. Model graphs,
  preprocessing, backend policy, cache ownership, and numeric postprocessing
  stay in AICore.
- Link `AICore` and include only public `aicore/*` headers. Never include ggml or
  task-private headers from a plugin.
- Register the plugin option/target in the central plugin CMake entry, ship
  `info.json`/resources/README, and add incremental `BUILD.md` and plugin-index
  rows. When automation is in scope, extend the existing JSON-RPC and
  `cli-anything-acloudviewer` mapping/tests rather than adding private CLI flags.
- Reuse `ecvAICoreRuntimeHelpers.h`, `ecvAICoreUiHelper.h`, shared video
  components, `ecvTestDataRepository`, and the AICore-exported model catalog.
  Never add plugin-private device locks, model URL/digest tables, downloader
  implementations, cache roots, or decoded-image preprocessing pipelines.
  Plugins may feed exported catalog metadata to the shared
  `ecvModelDownloader` / `ecvAssetIntegrity` services.
- Worker results carry source identity and generation. Contexts stay on the
  worker thread; pending teardown follows the shared helper. Live pipelines
  keep one running frame plus at most one latest pending frame and call
  `completeFrameProcessing()` on every completion path.
- Use borrowed `aicore_image_view` with the real `bytesPerLine()` and format;
  use typed results with their documented release function. File, encoded-image,
  and JSON round-trips are compatibility/export paths, not frame inference.
- Plugin helper tests do not replace the headless task gate. Verify the actual
  still/live/cancel/stale/error/teardown workflow after AICore truth, parity,
  repeated-forward, and validation-manifest coverage pass.
- Run `python3 core/AICore/tests/check_plugin_aicore_boundaries.py --root .
  --strict`; it must pass repository-wide, not only for new plugins.

## Third-Party Dependencies and Qt UI Frameworks

- Before adding or porting a dependency, read the existing
  [reuse/ownership contract](<../skills/acloudviewer-aicore-plugin/references/reuse-contract.md>)
  and inspect the active [dependency registration](<../../3rdparty/find_dependencies.cmake>),
  [libpng recipe](<../../3rdparty/libpng/libpng.cmake>) and a relevant existing
  patch recipe. Extend the existing owner/target; do not invent a second loader,
  source-of-truth, private cache or per-plugin copy of a shared framework.
- Use `3rdparty/<dep>/<dep>.cmake` with `ExternalProject_Add`, a pinned upstream
  URL/version/SHA-256, the shared download cache, `UPDATE_COMMAND ""`, declared
  dependencies/byproducts and the established install/export targets. The default
  stays fixed; upgrades are explicit reviewed pin/digest/patch/test changes, never
  floating HEAD. Do not store upstream source tarballs in the repository or add
  custom unpack/add_subdirectory machinery in an application library.
- Qt5/Qt6 compatibility belongs in ordered Git patches applied dynamically by
  CMake `PATCH_COMMAND` to the extracted dependency source. Use
  `git apply --no-index --check` before apply, with the exact source working
  directory; verify every required result after apply and on already-applied
  reuse against current patch identity/content. Neither exit 0, one marker nor
  a prior build/done stamp is sufficient. Handle overlapping patches with a
  complete final-state check; fail on missing patches, partial application,
  source drift or conflicts. No shell `patch`, production edits of extracted
  trees, or "already applied or not applicable" fallback. Existing runners are
  evidence to inspect, not permission to copy weak skip branches.
- Reuse `QT_PREFIX`, `QT_VERSION_MAJOR` and `Qt::` targets already selected by
  `cmake/CMakeExternalLibs.cmake`. A dependency recipe must not repeat
  `if(USE_QT6)` selection or outer `find_package(Qt5/Qt6)`. Only its independent
  ExternalProject configure needs package discovery, constrained to the exact
  Qt package paths forwarded by the parent, not a second version policy.
- Propagate the parent's compiler/launcher/toolchain, C++ standard, Qt major and
  component paths, macOS architectures/sysroot/deployment target, configuration
  and MSVC CRT. Preserve list-valued arguments. Declare real platform static
  names/suffixes, Debug postfixes, PIC, export headers, byproducts and installed
  target/dependency closure. Bound nested build/autogen parallelism by the
  authorized overall job budget; do not reset parent policy inside the library.
- Sample/test-data actions are an explicitly approved semantic emphasis, not
  disposable generic chrome. Keep their prominent style in Native and Custom;
  create them through `ecvAICoreUi::makeSampleDataBtn` so state colors are owned
  once. Do not duplicate per-plugin QSS or remove the emphasis while consolidating
  ordinary control styles; do not apply it to every Browse/Load/Segment action.
- For toolbar density regressions, compare identical actions, icon sizes,
  font/DPI, window width and layout state against Fusion. Measure tool-button
  size hints, split-menu subcontrols and toolbar margins/spacing/separators;
  fix the geometry owner, not icons or per-widget max-size QSS. Painting,
  hit-testing and menu indicators must use the same compact subcontrol contract.
- Verify fresh/reused sources, patch/pin changes and conflict rejection, then
  real consumer build/link/install and relevant runtime tests. For UI frameworks
  exercise dense forms, popup/input/focus/disabled/local palette, animations,
  lifecycle and Native/Custom exit; preserve approved artwork and plugin-local
  resources. Report exact Qt/OS/compiler/DPI and untested platforms. Do not claim
  arbitrary upstream upgrades apply without conflicts or Qt5 evidence proves
  Qt6/cross-platform runtime/ABI compatibility. Pure UI dependencies do not
  require unrelated AICore numerical gates. See the
  [Qt framework integration gate](<../skills/logo-design/references/functional-icon-verification.md#third-party-qt-framework-integration-gate>).

## Key Interfaces

- `ccStdPluginInterface` — base class for standard plugins
- `ecvMainAppInterface` — access to app (DB, views, console, selection)
- `ccCommandLineInterface` — register CLI commands via `registerCommand()`
- `ecvDisplayTools` — static display operations (redraw, view matrix, etc.)

## Common Patterns

- Get app interface: `m_app` (set via `setMainAppInterface()`)
- Access DB root: `m_app->dbRootObject()`
- Cast entities: `ccHObjectCaster::ToPointCloud(obj)`, `ccHObjectCaster::ToMesh(obj)`
- Force redraw: `ecvDisplayTools::RedrawDisplay()`
- View orientations: `CC_TOP_VIEW`, `CC_FRONT_VIEW`, `CC_ISO_VIEW_1`, etc.

## Build

```bash
cmake -DPLUGIN_STANDARD_QJSONRPC=ON -DPLUGIN_STANDARD_QSIBR=ON ..
make -j$(nproc)
```

## Entity Types

| Enum | Type |
|------|------|
| `CV_TYPES::POINT_CLOUD` | Point cloud |
| `CV_TYPES::MESH` | Triangle mesh |
| `CV_TYPES::POLY_LINE` | Polyline |
| `CV_TYPES::HIERARCHY_OBJECT` | Group/container |
